  <h1>Neural Networks in Quant &amp; Finance</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Why this matters for <strong>HFT_practice</strong>: there's no PyTorch in the repo
    today — and your flagship gradient booster (Lesson 0003) is the right default for
    daily tabular alpha. But a neural net is <em>already</em> silently feeding your
    pipeline: Alpha Vantage's sentiment score in <code>news_features.py</code> is the
    output of someone else's transformer. Knowing where NNs win, where they lose, and
    where they'd fit here lets you make that call deliberately.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §1 WHAT IS A NN ===================== -->
  <h2>1. What a neural network actually is</h2>

  <p>
    Strip away the marketing and a neural network is a stack of two operations,
    repeated:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>A <strong>linear transform</strong>: <code>z = W·x + b</code></li>
    <li>A <strong>non-linearity</strong> applied elementwise: <code>h = σ(z)</code> (ReLU, sigmoid, tanh, GELU)</li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    Stack <em>L</em> of those, train all the W's and b's so the output matches a
    target, and you've got a feedforward neural network with <em>L</em> layers.
    The flexibility comes from the non-linearity — without it, ten stacked linear
    layers collapse to one linear layer.
  </p>

  <div class="nn-diagram">
    <div class="layer">
      <div class="layer-name">Input</div>
      <div class="neurons">
        <div class="neuron"></div><div class="neuron"></div><div class="neuron"></div>
        <div class="neuron"></div><div class="neuron"></div><div class="neuron"></div>
        <div class="neuron"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="layer-desc">7 features (the same ones your GBM sees)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="layer">
      <div class="layer-name">Hidden 1 + ReLU</div>
      <div class="neurons">
        <div class="neuron"></div><div class="neuron"></div><div class="neuron"></div>
        <div class="neuron"></div><div class="neuron"></div><div class="neuron"></div>
        <div class="neuron"></div><div class="neuron"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="layer-desc">W₁·x + b₁, then ReLU</div>
    </div>
    <div class="layer">
      <div class="layer-name">Hidden 2 + ReLU</div>
      <div class="neurons">
        <div class="neuron"></div><div class="neuron"></div><div class="neuron"></div>
        <div class="neuron"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="layer-desc">W₂·h₁ + b₂, then ReLU</div>
    </div>
    <div class="layer">
      <div class="layer-name">Output</div>
      <div class="neurons">
        <div class="neuron"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="layer-desc">W₃·h₂ + b₃ → fwd_ret_1d</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    The training algorithm — <strong>backpropagation + gradient descent</strong> — is
    just the chain rule applied to the loss function plus a numerical optimizer.
    Same family as Lesson 0003's gradient boosting (both use gradients), totally
    different mechanics (boosting builds trees; NN updates weights in place).
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>The Universal Approximation Theorem</strong> says a network with one
    sufficiently wide hidden layer can approximate any continuous function. In
    practice that's a theoretical comfort — depth (more layers) matters more than
    width for actually learning useful representations from finite data.
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §2 MINIMAL EXAMPLE ===================== -->
  <h2>2. A 20-line standalone example</h2>

  <p>
    A neural net trying to do exactly what your <code>GradientBoostingRegressor</code>
    does — predict <code>fwd_ret_1d</code> from the 7 features:
  </p>

<pre><span class="kw">import</span> torch
<span class="kw">import</span> torch.nn <span class="kw">as</span> nn

<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="ty">ReturnMLP</span>(nn.Module):
    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__init__</span>(self, n_features: <span class="ty">int</span> = <span class="num">7</span>):
        <span class="fn">super</span>().<span class="fn">__init__</span>()
        self.net = nn.<span class="fn">Sequential</span>(
            nn.<span class="fn">Linear</span>(n_features, <span class="num">32</span>),   <span class="com"># W₁ x + b₁ — input → 32 hidden</span>
            nn.<span class="fn">ReLU</span>(),                  <span class="com"># non-linearity</span>
            nn.<span class="fn">Linear</span>(<span class="num">32</span>, <span class="num">16</span>),           <span class="com"># W₂ h₁ + b₂ — 32 → 16</span>
            nn.<span class="fn">ReLU</span>(),
            nn.<span class="fn">Linear</span>(<span class="num">16</span>, <span class="num">1</span>),            <span class="com"># W₃ h₂ + b₃ — scalar return prediction</span>
        )
    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">forward</span>(self, x):
        <span class="kw">return</span> self.net(x).<span class="fn">squeeze</span>(-<span class="num">1</span>)

model = <span class="fn">ReturnMLP</span>()
opt   = torch.optim.<span class="fn">Adam</span>(model.<span class="fn">parameters</span>(), lr=<span class="num">1e-3</span>)
loss_fn = nn.<span class="fn">MSELoss</span>()           <span class="com"># same squared-error target as the GBM</span>

<span class="kw">for</span> epoch <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">range</span>(<span class="num">50</span>):
    pred = <span class="fn">model</span>(X_train)         <span class="com"># forward pass</span>
    loss = <span class="fn">loss_fn</span>(pred, y_train)
    opt.<span class="fn">zero_grad</span>(); loss.<span class="fn">backward</span>(); opt.<span class="fn">step</span>()   <span class="com"># backprop + update</span>
</pre>

  <p>
    Notice what this gives you for free that the GBM doesn't:
    <strong>differentiability end-to-end</strong> — you can plug this model into
    a bigger system that's also differentiable (a downstream RL agent, a
    differentiable risk constraint) and train it all jointly. The GBM is a
    sealed box; the NN is a Lego brick.
  </p>

  <p>
    And notice what it gives you <em>nothing</em> of: out-of-the-box robustness on
    7 tabular features and ~10,000 rows. The GBM will outperform this MLP almost
    every time on that data regime. Neural nets shine where they have <em>much more
    data</em>, <em>much richer inputs</em> (sequences, text, images), or both.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §3 FIVE APPLICATIONS ===================== -->
  <h2>3. Five places neural networks actually win in quant</h2>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>1. Limit-order-book microstructure (DeepLOB / TransLOB / TLOB)</h4>
      <p>
        Each LOB snapshot is a 2D grid: 10 price levels × {bid_size, ask_size}, over
        time. CNNs (DeepLOB, 2018) extract local price-level patterns; transformers
        (TransLOB, TLOB 2025) capture longer-range dynamics. This is the
        <strong>natural NN bridge to your FPGA work</strong>: replace
        <code>market_maker.vhd</code>'s hand-tuned spread logic with a quantized
        NN trained on LOB snapshots.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>2. Sequence forecasting (LSTM / GRU / Transformer)</h4>
      <p>
        For multi-step price/volatility forecasting at intraday or daily horizons.
        LSTMs/GRUs were the dominant choice 2016–2022; transformers and the
        2024–2025 foundation models (TimesFM, Chronos, Kronos — Lesson 0005)
        are now state-of-the-art on benchmarks. <em>Not yet</em> consistently
        beating well-tuned GBM on tabular cross-sectional equity alpha.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>3. Text / sentiment (BERT, FinBERT)</h4>
      <p>
        Where NNs <strong>flat-out dominate</strong>. Earnings call transcripts,
        SEC filings, news headlines — all unstructured text that a transformer
        encodes into vectors that downstream models can use. Alpha Vantage's
        <code>sentiment</code> score is a number produced by exactly this kind
        of model.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>4. Deep reinforcement learning (execution &amp; market making)</h4>
      <p>
        Learn an action policy from reward (P&amp;L − inventory penalty − cost).
        Useful for execution algorithms (DDQN-style splitting of a parent order)
        and academic market-making research. Production deployments are
        cautious because RL is notoriously brittle and hard to backtest honestly.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>5. Neural volatility and option pricing</h4>
      <p>
        Replace closed-form models (Black–Scholes, Heston) with NNs that learn
        the implied-vol surface or option price directly from market quotes.
        Faster inference (no PDE solve at runtime), arbitrage-respecting
        architectures exist. Used in pricing engines, not return prediction.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>The honest summary</h4>
      <p>
        On tabular daily features: GBM wins. On raw LOB or text: NN wins. On
        sequence forecasting: jury still out, GBM is the safe default. RL is
        promising-but-hard. Vol/options: NN now mainstream. Map every NN proposal
        you see onto this grid before getting excited.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §4 IN THIS PROJECT ===================== -->
  <h2>4. In this project — where NNs hide and where they could fit</h2>

  <h3>(a) The hidden NN: Alpha Vantage sentiment</h3>
  <span class="filepath">news_features.py (lines 32–34)</span>
<pre><span class="com"># Coerce numeric fields</span>
<span class="kw">for</span> c <span class="kw">in</span> (<span class="str">"sentiment"</span>, <span class="str">"relevance"</span>):
    <span class="kw">if</span> c <span class="kw">in</span> df.columns:
        df[c] = pd.<span class="fn">to_numeric</span>(df[c], errors=<span class="str">"coerce"</span>)
</pre>
  <p>
    That <code>sentiment</code> column is a float in roughly [−1, +1]. <strong>You
    didn't compute it.</strong> Alpha Vantage's NEWS_SENTIMENT endpoint runs a
    BERT-family transformer on each article and returns a pre-aggregated score.
    Your pipeline already includes neural-net output — you just don't own the
    model, the training data, or the failure modes.
  </p>

  <span class="filepath">news_features.py (lines 53–58)</span>
<pre>daily = grp.<span class="fn">agg</span>(
    news_count=(<span class="str">"ticker"</span>, <span class="str">"count"</span>),
    sent_mean=(<span class="str">"sentiment"</span>, <span class="str">"mean"</span>),
    sent_abs_mean=(<span class="str">"sentiment"</span>, <span class="kw">lambda</span> s: np.<span class="fn">nanmean</span>(np.<span class="fn">abs</span>(s))),
    relevance_sum=(<span class="str">"relevance"</span>, <span class="str">"sum"</span>),
)
</pre>
  <p>
    You then aggregate those NN outputs into daily features (mean, abs-mean,
    rolling sums) that get merged into the GBM. So the project's effective
    architecture is: <strong>someone else's transformer → your hand-built
    aggregations → your GBM → your portfolio</strong>. A real upgrade path is
    to replace step 1 with a model you own (FinBERT fine-tuned on financial
    text), so you control the failure modes.
  </p>

  <h3>(b) Where you could (but don't) put an NN of your own</h3>
  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Replace the GBM with an MLP — <em>probably not worth it</em></h4>
      <p>
        7 tabular features, ~10⁴ rows: a 2-layer MLP will likely match the GBM,
        not beat it. Lots of code complexity for no win.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Replace the GBM with a transformer over a price sequence — <em>defensible experiment</em></h4>
      <p>
        Feed the last 60 days of OHLCV per ticker as a sequence to a small
        transformer. May capture momentum / mean-reversion patterns the GBM misses.
        Honest comparison requires walk-forward, which <code>walkforward_sim.py</code>
        already supports.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>FinBERT-fine-tuned text encoder — <em>highest expected value</em></h4>
      <p>
        Bypass Alpha Vantage's sentiment, score raw articles yourself with a
        domain-tuned model. You'd own the signal and could pre-compute embeddings
        instead of a single scalar.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Quantized NN on the FPGA via hls4ml — <em>the connection to Lesson 0002</em></h4>
      <p>
        Train a tiny MLP (~1k weights, INT8 quantized), compile it with
        <strong>hls4ml</strong> into VHDL alongside <code>linear_regression_predictor.vhd</code>.
        The README's "ML inference pipeline" roadmap item is exactly this.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Repo honesty:</strong> none of these NN extensions are wired up.
    The flagship ~27% number comes from the GBM. Don't let "neural net" excitement
    push you to abandon a proven model on a dataset where it's the right choice —
    add NNs where they have a genuine edge (text, LOB), not as a status upgrade.
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §5 CONTRASTS ===================== -->
  <h2>5. Often confused with</h2>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>NN vs Gradient Boosting (on tabular data)</h4>
      <p>
        Both fit non-linear functions. <strong>GBM</strong> excels with few features,
        moderate data, easy hyperparameter tuning, fast training, robust defaults.
        <strong>NN</strong> excels with many features, lots of data, end-to-end
        differentiability, raw inputs (no feature engineering). On 7 daily
        features × 10k rows, GBM wins.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>RNN (LSTM/GRU) vs Transformer</h4>
      <p>
        Both handle sequences. <strong>RNN</strong> processes step-by-step,
        carries hidden state forward, struggles with very long contexts.
        <strong>Transformer</strong> attends to all positions at once via
        self-attention, parallelizes well, dominates at long-range.
        For daily OHLCV over 1–2 years (252–504 steps), either works;
        transformer is the modern default.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Supervised vs Reinforcement Learning</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Supervised</strong>: labelled (x, y) pairs, minimize prediction
        loss. <strong>RL</strong>: learn an action policy from a reward signal
        in an environment. The repo's GBM is supervised; a market-making policy
        on top of <code>market_maker.vhd</code> would be RL.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Pretraining vs Fine-tuning</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Pretraining</strong>: train a large model on a huge generic
        corpus (BERT on Wikipedia, TimesFM on 100B time-series points).
        <strong>Fine-tuning</strong>: take that pretrained model and adapt
        it to your specific task with much less data. FinBERT is BERT fine-tuned
        on financial text.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Activation vs Loss function</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Activation</strong> (ReLU, sigmoid, GELU) — the elementwise
        non-linearity inside each layer. <strong>Loss</strong> (MSE, cross-entropy,
        Huber) — the scalar objective the optimizer minimizes. Different roles;
        both involve non-linear functions; constantly confused.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Epoch vs Batch vs Iteration</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Batch</strong>: a chunk of training rows used for one gradient
        step. <strong>Iteration</strong>: one batch processed. <strong>Epoch</strong>:
        one full pass through the entire training set. With 10k rows and batch
        size 100, one epoch = 100 iterations.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

