    <p class="q">Q2. Your colleague proposes replacing <code>GradientBoostingRegressor</code> in <code>paper_trading_sim.py</code> with a 3-layer MLP on the same 7 features. Most likely outcome?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="That's a common assumption but it's mostly wrong on small tabular data. NNs need either far more data, far richer inputs, or both, to consistently outperform a well-tuned GBM.">The MLP outperforms the GBM by a wide margin because neural networks are universally more powerful.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. On ~10⁴ rows × 7 features — a small tabular regime — well-tuned GBMs almost always match or beat MLPs. The NN brings differentiability that you don't need here, and a lot more hyperparameter fiddling. No expected edge; lots of complexity added.">The MLP roughly matches the GBM but adds a lot of complexity for no measurable predictive gain.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="The training pipeline is straightforward — it's the predictive performance that's the issue. Modern frameworks make NN training easy; the problem is generalization on small tabular data.">The MLP can't be trained at all because the dataset is too small for backprop.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="No — sklearn's GradientBoostingRegressor doesn't expose gradients to downstream PyTorch code. Differentiability is a real benefit of NNs in pipelines that need it, but this repo's pipeline doesn't.">The MLP automatically makes the rest of the pipeline differentiable, which is a strict upgrade.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q3. Why is the FPGA roadmap's "ML inference pipeline" item specifically pointing at <strong>hls4ml</strong> rather than at hand-written VHDL?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Both can express the math. The issue isn't whether VHDL can — it's how much human effort it takes for a multi-layer quantized model.">Because VHDL cannot express the matrix multiplications needed for a neural network.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Right. hls4ml takes a trained Keras/sklearn/PyTorch model and generates the HDL for you, including the quantization and parallelization decisions. Hand-coding a quantized MLP in VHDL is doable but tedious; hls4ml automates it and is the documented bridge from sklearn to FPGA in the repo's README.">Because hls4ml automates converting a trained quantized neural net into HDL — hand-writing the multi-layer pipeline in VHDL would be tedious and error-prone for anything but the smallest networks.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="hls4ml is open-source and free; cost isn't the reason. It's chosen because it bridges Python-trained models into HDL automatically.">Because hls4ml is the only free option for FPGA synthesis.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="hls4ml is widely used in academic and industrial HFT research. The reason for choosing it is automation, not popularity.">Because hls4ml is the only NN-to-FPGA toolchain anyone uses anymore.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q4. Which neural-network application is the strongest fit for direct deployment alongside <code>fpga/market_maker.vhd</code>?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="FinBERT works on news text. The FPGA hot path doesn't process text — it processes prices in nanoseconds. The model would never see its input on the right side of the latency budget.">A FinBERT fine-tuned on earnings calls to compute sentiment.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Correct. DeepLOB/TransLOB/TLOB train on raw LOB snapshots (bid/ask sizes and prices at each level) to predict short-term price moves. That's exactly the data the FPGA already has at line rate. A quantized small variant could plug in next to the market_maker module via hls4ml.">A DeepLOB- or TransLOB-style network trained on raw limit-order-book snapshots, quantized and compiled to HDL via hls4ml.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="A 200M-parameter foundation model can't fit on an FPGA, and its inference latency would blow the entire tick-to-trade budget. These models are CPU/GPU-only at inference time.">A full TimesFM or Chronos foundation model loaded into BRAM.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Deep RL has promising research applications but is too unstable to deploy directly on silicon for live trading without a CPU-side safety net.">A deep RL agent trained end-to-end with live exchange P&L as the reward.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <div class="question" data-question>
    <p class="q">Q5. A teammate says "let's stack ten linear layers without activations, since linear is fast on the FPGA." What's wrong with this plan?</p>
    <ul class="options">
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Memory isn't the issue on an FPGA for ten small linear layers. The issue is mathematical: without nonlinearities the composition collapses.">FPGAs can't store ten weight matrices at once.</li>
      <li data-correct="true"  data-why="Right. The composition of linear functions is itself linear. Ten linear layers without nonlinearity in between equal one linear layer, with the same expressive power as your existing linear_regression_predictor.vhd. The nonlinearity is what gives multi-layer networks their power.">Ten stacked linear layers without a nonlinearity collapse mathematically to a single linear layer — you get no extra expressive power, just more weights to store.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="It might overfit, but only because you have more parameters than needed. The bigger structural problem is that those parameters are mathematically redundant.">It would overfit catastrophically due to too many parameters.</li>
      <li data-correct="false" data-why="Backpropagation handles arbitrary linear architectures fine. The math problem is upstream of training — the model is equivalent to a one-layer model regardless of how it's trained.">Backpropagation can't compute gradients through a network without ReLU.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="feedback" hidden></p>
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §7 SCENARIOS ===================== -->
  <h2>7. Check your understanding (scenarios)</h2>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S1. A teammate wants to "improve the news features by fine-tuning FinBERT on our own data." How does this change the pipeline conceptually, and what's the upside over the current approach?</summary>
    <p>
      Today the pipeline is: Alpha Vantage transformer → scalar sentiment per article →
      your aggregations → GBM. With a fine-tuned FinBERT you'd own step 1: feed raw
      headlines/articles into a model you control, emit either your own sentiment
      scalar or, better, a learned <em>embedding</em> vector that captures more
      nuance than a single number. Upsides: control over training data and bias,
      ability to emit richer features (per-article embeddings the GBM can use as
      additional columns), and the ability to retrain when the world changes
      (post-election, post-crisis). Downside: you now own the failure modes too —
      including the GPU bill and the labeled-data problem.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S2. You train a transformer on 5 years of AAPL minute bars and it reports 92% directional accuracy on the test set. Should you ship it?</summary>
    <p>
      Almost certainly not without serious scrutiny. Three failure modes to rule out
      first: (1) <strong>Look-ahead bias</strong> — does any feature contain information
      from after the prediction time? Easy to introduce with rolling normalizations.
      (2) <strong>Test-set contamination</strong> — did the model see overlapping
      windows during training? Common with sliding-window setups. (3)
      <strong>Survivorship and data quality</strong> — minute bars on a single
      surviving large-cap drastically overstate strategy quality.
      92% on directional accuracy is a number so high it's almost certainly a bug;
      real intraday directional models are happy at 53–55%. The lesson is to trust
      the methodology, not the headline number.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S3. The Universal Approximation Theorem says one hidden layer is enough. Why do real networks use ten or more?</summary>
    <p>
      Because the theorem is about <em>existence</em>, not <em>learnability</em>.
      A single-hidden-layer network <em>could</em> represent the target function,
      but might need exponentially many neurons to do so, and gradient descent has
      no efficient path to find those weights. Deep networks decompose the problem
      into a hierarchy of representations — early layers learn local patterns,
      later layers compose them — which is far more sample-efficient. The theorem
      is comfort, not a recipe.
    </p>
  </details>

